WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.841
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:00.841 --> 00:00:02.340
LIZ FONG-JONES:
Welcome to episode 9

00:00:02.340 --> 00:00:04.190
of our series on SRE and DevOps.

00:00:04.190 --> 00:00:06.779
I'm Liz Fong-Jones, a site
reliability engineer at Google,

00:00:06.779 --> 00:00:08.820
where I teach Google Cloud
customers how to build

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:10.364
and operate reliable services.

00:00:10.364 --> 00:00:12.030
SETH: And I'm Seth,
a developer advocate

00:00:12.030 --> 00:00:14.219
at Google focused on
infrastructure and operations.

00:00:14.219 --> 00:00:15.760
LIZ FONG-JONES: So
Seth, I was hoping

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:17.259
you would help me
figure out why are

00:00:17.259 --> 00:00:19.560
things so chaotic every time
one of my services break?

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:21.720
SETH: So what kind of
chaos are you experiencing?

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:22.587
Too much data?

00:00:22.587 --> 00:00:23.670
Slow internet connections?

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:24.570
Corporate firewalls?

00:00:24.570 --> 00:00:25.740
VPNs?

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:26.880
LIZ FONG-JONES: No, that's
not actually what I mean.

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:28.290
I'm talking about human chaos.

00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:31.290
There's often both too much
communication and too little

00:00:31.290 --> 00:00:32.640
communication at the same time.

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:34.230
Everyone wants to participate.

00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:36.720
People are doing random things
that don't actually help.

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:39.060
And people actually doing
the work aren't talking.

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:39.799
SETH: Ah, yes.

00:00:39.799 --> 00:00:42.090
Humans are definitely an
important part of the incident

00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:43.380
management process.

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:45.540
It sounds like you need
to define very clear roles

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:47.605
and responsibilities
for each of your humans,

00:00:47.605 --> 00:00:49.230
so that they know
exactly what they can

00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:50.740
do without getting in the way.

00:00:50.740 --> 00:00:51.880
LIZ FONG-JONES: I'd
love to figure that out.

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:53.010
How do I get started, Seth?

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:55.260
SETH: First, you'll
need a process

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:57.600
for declaring
incidents, a dashboard

00:00:57.600 --> 00:00:59.340
for viewing your
current incidents,

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:00.930
and some kind of
database for who

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:03.180
to contact when an
incident is in progress

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:04.650
for different
kinds of incidents.

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:06.570
You'll also need
to set thresholds

00:01:06.570 --> 00:01:08.580
for when to declare
an incident and who

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:10.990
can declare an incident
in your organization.

00:01:10.990 --> 00:01:12.660
Depending on the
scale of the incident,

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:14.534
you may also need to
bring in specialists

00:01:14.534 --> 00:01:16.200
from other parts of
the company in order

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:17.430
to help resolve the incident.

00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:17.790
LIZ FONG-JONES: Right.

00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:19.873
I wouldn't necessarily
want to declare an incident

00:01:19.873 --> 00:01:22.170
every single time and
get paged, but I also

00:01:22.170 --> 00:01:24.840
wanted to feel supported in
declaring an incident if I

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:26.250
need to bring in other people.

00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:26.830
Then what?

00:01:26.830 --> 00:01:29.130
SETH: So I mentioned
having someone to contact.

00:01:29.130 --> 00:01:31.860
The most critical role in
the incident response process

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:34.950
is that of incident
commander, or IC,

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:37.440
who is responsible for
making strategic decisions

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:40.650
and delegating additional
roles to other responders.

00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:42.990
The other roles can also
be played by the incident

00:01:42.990 --> 00:01:46.110
commander but tend to be
delegated as the incident grows

00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:47.966
in size or complexity.

00:01:47.966 --> 00:01:49.590
The first role that
tends to be created

00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:51.570
is that of the
operations lead, who

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:54.060
is in charge of gaining a
detailed understanding of

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:57.030
and actually changing
the state of the system--

00:01:57.030 --> 00:01:59.691
like running commands,
grabbing log files, et cetera.

00:01:59.691 --> 00:02:01.440
LIZ FONG-JONES: I
gather that only knowing

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:03.840
having one person touching the
system makes a lot of sense,

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:06.090
but I'm not sure how that's
helpful to have the incident

00:02:06.090 --> 00:02:07.470
commander isolated
from the details.

00:02:07.470 --> 00:02:09.120
Don't they need to
know all those details?

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:09.953
SETH: So yes and no.

00:02:09.953 --> 00:02:13.170
I mean, have you ever been
driving a car in the dark

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:15.609
in some foreign country on
the opposite side of the road,

00:02:15.609 --> 00:02:18.150
and you're trying to get where
you need to go, but you can't?

00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:19.524
LIZ FONG-JONES:
A few times, yes.

00:02:19.524 --> 00:02:21.450
And it's super
difficult. I usually

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:23.850
have to pull over because
if there's no one else

00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:26.070
in the car with me, I
can't both look at the map

00:02:26.070 --> 00:02:28.650
and figure out where am I
going, where am I trying to go.

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:30.660
And then, I drive
safely at the same time.

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:31.870
I can't do that all at once.

00:02:31.870 --> 00:02:32.130
SETH: Right.

00:02:32.130 --> 00:02:34.380
So I think you just answered
your own question, right?

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:36.930
If we apply that same analogy
to the operations world,

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:39.600
it's hard to both
plan and perform

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:42.180
to keep track of all of
the fine-grained details

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:43.060
at the same time.

00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:45.420
So it's usually best
to separate the two

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:47.624
roles to minimize the
overhead of context switching.

00:02:47.624 --> 00:02:48.540
LIZ FONG-JONES: I see.

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:49.430
That makes a lot of sense.

00:02:49.430 --> 00:02:52.150
So the incident commander and
the operations lead under them,

00:02:52.150 --> 00:02:53.200
but you mentioned
multiple roles.

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:55.050
What other roles might
an incident commander

00:02:55.050 --> 00:02:55.740
want to delegate?

00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:57.720
SETH: Well, have you ever
had external stakeholders,

00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:00.030
like a support team or
executives, who are bugging you

00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:01.140
for updates during an outage?

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:01.930
LIZ FONG-JONES: All the time.

00:03:01.930 --> 00:03:04.260
SETH: Or maybe if you're a
public-facing company, perhaps

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:05.610
you have a presence
on social media

00:03:05.610 --> 00:03:07.401
where your users are
constantly nagging you

00:03:07.401 --> 00:03:08.780
about the service being down.

00:03:08.780 --> 00:03:10.110
LIZ FONG-JONES: Ah, it's
super distracting, especially

00:03:10.110 --> 00:03:11.490
if I'm the operations lead.

00:03:11.490 --> 00:03:13.800
But isn't that just the
incident commander's job to do?

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:15.150
SETH: Sometimes--
up to the point

00:03:15.150 --> 00:03:17.358
that the incident commander
can no longer effectively

00:03:17.358 --> 00:03:20.280
do both the planning and
the communications work.

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:22.680
At that point, you'll
want a communications lead

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:25.181
for communicating with other
teams or external stakeholders.

00:03:25.181 --> 00:03:26.096
LIZ FONG-JONES: I see.

00:03:26.096 --> 00:03:27.780
And what if an
incident grows so large

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:29.520
that the decision
making responsibility,

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:31.380
and the planning, and the
logistical work becomes

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:32.830
too much work for
one person to handle?

00:03:32.830 --> 00:03:33.430
Then what?

00:03:33.430 --> 00:03:34.530
SETH: So in that
scenario, you might

00:03:34.530 --> 00:03:36.480
want to have a dedicated
planning lead--

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:38.670
having someone be in
charge of writing the plan,

00:03:38.670 --> 00:03:40.680
maintaining the
running notes document,

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:42.870
and starting to draft
the post-mortem.

00:03:42.870 --> 00:03:44.910
Another common role
is a logistics lead,

00:03:44.910 --> 00:03:47.260
who handles things
like securing rooms,

00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:49.360
making sure that people
have food and water,

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:50.490
and other supplies.

00:03:50.490 --> 00:03:52.284
Or you could get more
creative, depending

00:03:52.284 --> 00:03:54.450
on the needs of your
organization and your business.

00:03:54.450 --> 00:03:55.750
LIZ FONG-JONES: That
makes a lot of sense.

00:03:55.750 --> 00:03:57.990
What can't the incident
commander delegate, though?

00:03:57.990 --> 00:03:59.100
It sounds like the
incident commander

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:00.139
can delegate anything.

00:04:00.139 --> 00:04:01.680
SETH: So the incident
commander can't

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:04.650
delegate overall
decision-making responsibility.

00:04:04.650 --> 00:04:06.330
The buck stops with them.

00:04:06.330 --> 00:04:08.610
They have to help their
subordinates make decisions,

00:04:08.610 --> 00:04:10.860
tiebreak in the event
that people can't agree,

00:04:10.860 --> 00:04:13.530
and coordinate adding new people
into the incident response

00:04:13.530 --> 00:04:15.240
hierarchy as deemed appropriate.

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:15.900
LIZ FONG-JONES:
And what if there

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:18.209
needs to be multiple people
working hands on keyboard,

00:04:18.209 --> 00:04:20.629
or multiple people need to
be doing the communications?

00:04:20.629 --> 00:04:21.420
How does that work?

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:22.590
I thought you said
only one person's

00:04:22.590 --> 00:04:23.560
in charge of operations.

00:04:23.560 --> 00:04:25.320
SETH: So additional
subject matter experts

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:27.240
can join under the
operations lead.

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:28.890
Or the overall
communications lead

00:04:28.890 --> 00:04:31.500
might want to designate someone
to be on the customer support

00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:34.200
team, while another person
is handling social media

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:35.760
for external communications.

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:37.220
LIZ FONG-JONES: And what
about the role of managers

00:04:37.220 --> 00:04:37.950
and technical leads?

00:04:37.950 --> 00:04:39.950
Do I automatically become
the incident commander

00:04:39.950 --> 00:04:42.270
just because I'm the most
senior person who turned up?

00:04:42.270 --> 00:04:44.520
SETH: No, not unless you're
specifically asked to.

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:45.840
The person who
declared the incident

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:47.640
is always the incident
commander until they

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:49.110
designate a successor.

00:04:49.110 --> 00:04:51.300
It's up to them to decide
what roles in which

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:54.210
to place people joining
or even turn people away

00:04:54.210 --> 00:04:56.310
if there are too many
cooks in the kitchen.

00:04:56.310 --> 00:04:58.500
For long running incidents,
the incident commander

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:01.410
made delegate a successor in
a more appropriate time zone

00:05:01.410 --> 00:05:02.700
or geography.

00:05:02.700 --> 00:05:05.340
As an incident evolves
in size or complexity,

00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:08.880
some incident commanders may
want to give up the role of IC

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:10.630
and instead become
the operations lead.

00:05:10.630 --> 00:05:10.980
LIZ FONG-JONES: Great.

00:05:10.980 --> 00:05:12.938
That should help clear
up some of the confusion

00:05:12.938 --> 00:05:14.379
and chaos for my next incident.

00:05:14.379 --> 00:05:15.670
Thanks, everyone, for watching.

00:05:15.670 --> 00:05:17.170
And we hope you'll
tune in next time

00:05:17.170 --> 00:05:19.110
to learn what to do
after an incident.

00:05:19.110 --> 00:05:21.370
To find out when we post
our upcoming episodes,

00:05:21.370 --> 00:05:23.250
please like and subscribe below.

00:05:23.250 --> 00:05:25.700
[MUSIC PLAYING]

